gawk 5.4.1: an array element first used as a number or a condition prints "0" instead of ""

5.4.1 turns a new (untyped) array element into a scalar as soon as it is used (eval.c
elem_new_to_scalar() sets Node_val before unref, test mdim9), but elem_new_reset() before it
clears fields that share their memory with stfmt, and force_string_fmt() of 5.4.1 reformats a
value whose stfmt is not STFMT_UNUSED from its number. So

    BEGIN { if (!a["k"]) x = 1; print "[" a["k"] "]" }

prints [0], and libpng's scripts/options.awk writes a pnglibconf.h without PNG_READ_SUPPORTED
and PNG_SETJMP_SUPPORTED (the native build of libpng 1.6.59 fails). An untyped element holds the
null string: STFMT_UNUSED again wherever it becomes a value. The test suite of gawk passes.

--- a/awk.h
+++ b/awk.h
@@ -2043,6 +2043,7 @@
 	if (s->type == Node_elem_new) {
 		elem_new_reset(s);
 		s->type = Node_val;
+		s->stfmt = STFMT_UNUSED;	/* elem_new_reset() may clobber it */
 
 		return s;
 	}
--- a/eval.c
+++ b/eval.c
@@ -1952,6 +1952,7 @@
 	elem_new_reset(n);
 
 	n->type = Node_val;
+	n->stfmt = STFMT_UNUSED;	/* elem_new_reset() may clobber it */
 
 	if (n->valref > 1) {
 		unref(n);
--- a/node.c
